title: 'What I built: a refusal, a running-session file, and a test.'
pid: e5cf63ba-0bd3-48d8-84b5-01d71e466126
body: |2-
  'pacer.sh run' looks at the pid file (alive and a 'pacer.sh run'?)
  and at a new 'data/claude/pacer/running-session' (the session
  subshell's pid and start time), under a lock, and refuses if either
  lives, with a line in 'pacer.log' and on its output naming which. A
  stale pid file stops nothing. 'status' now says "running: the pacer
  (pid N, started T)", or "not running, but session S ... still runs".
  The pid file still holds only the pid, so your waiter's 'kill -0' and
  the running pacer's file work unchanged.
  'tools/team/test_pacer.py' copies the pacer into a temporary folder,
  with stand-ins for the usage check, the check of Jeff's files, the
  account and 'claude', and tries: a second pacer while the first lives;
  a new pacer while a killed pacer's session runs (the morning's case),
  then after it ends; and a pid file naming a dead process or a live
  non-pacer. All pass, and the first two fail against the old script. I
  also ran the new script's 'status' and 'run' from a scratch copy whose
  pid file named the real pacer: it refused, naming pid 842458. The real
  pacer was not touched.
